    "q-336900": {
      "dom_id": "step-q336900",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)+2y(t)-6 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=x(t)-3y(t)+{y}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the corresponding linearized system at the equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}=[2,\\text{ }1]^{T}?$\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" height=\"30px\" align=\"center\">${u}^{′}=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td width=\"5px\" height=\"40px\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td width=\"50px\" align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td width=\"50px\" align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td width=\"5px\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" height=\"30px\" align=\"center\">$\\,\\,u,\\,$ where $u=x-{x}^{∗}$</td>\n\n</tr>\n\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td width=\"30px\" align=\"center\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n\n</tbody></table>",
      "worked_solution": "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$f(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2y-6 \\\\ x-3y+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2y-6)=2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2y-6)=2 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x-3y+{y}^{2})=1,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x-3y+{y}^{2})=-3+2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}2x & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2y-3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}2(2) & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2(1)-3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, near the equilibrium, the nonlinear system is approximated by\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]u,\n$$\n\nwhere the deviation vector is\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x-2 \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
