    "q-337695": {
      "dom_id": "step-q337695",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+y(t)+x(t)^{3} \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-2x(t)+y(t)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the behavior of the system's solutions near the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\text{ }0]^{T}?$$\\,$\nThe equilibrium is an \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}.",
      "worked_solution": "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$f(x)=[\\begin{aligned}2x+y+{x}^{3} \\\\ -2x+{y}^{3}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =2+3{x}^{2},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =-2,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}2+3{x}^{2} & 1 \\\\ -2 & 3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\text{ }0]^{T}$ to get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}2+0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -2 & -\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(-\\lambda )+2 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=1\\pm i.$\n\nSince we have two complex eigenvalues with positive real parts, our equilibrium is an $\\text{unstable}$ $\\text{spiral source}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
